      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Knowing a being’s true name acts as a key that lets a caster direct magical energy specifically at that being.",
      "evidence": "In the same sources, spells that affect a target often require the target’s true name to bypass protections or distance, e.g., binding spells in Earthsea or name‑based compulsion spells in D&D.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The general effect of having that key is the ability to cast any spell on the person whose true name is known.",
      "evidence": "Examples include using a true name to cast curses, healing, protection, or domination; the true name is not limited to a single spell type but is the conduit for all magical effects.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Option E (“You can use a person's true name to cast spells on them”) captures this broad capability, whereas the other options describe either specific subsets (A, D, F, B) or a non‑magical use (C).",
      "evidence": "A, D, F are particular spell outcomes; B is not a standard function; C refers to mundane database lookup, which is unrelated to the mythic concept.",
